\par\par \b \uc2 Colombian Coffee Growers Demand Stronger  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Molinski   \par \uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2012\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MANIZALES, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's coffee sector Tuesday joined a chorus of critics of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  who feel the institution isn't doing enough to prevent excessive strength in the peso.\par \par \uc2 Mario Gomez, a longtime board member of the National Federation of Coffee Growers, said the bank should "at least double" its daily dollar purchases aimed at offsetting gains in the peso against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently buys a minimum of $20 million a day in the spot market.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian government and some bank board members, including Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry, have been urging for months for the bank to buy more dollars.\par \par \uc2 But so far the majority of the seven-member board resisted such a move, arguing it is important to keep a hands-off approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as much as possible to avoid inflationary pressures and other economic imbalances.\par \par \uc2 "The bank should be buying more dollars," Mr. Gomez said on the sidelines of a tourism-promotion event in the central, coffee-farming region city of Manizales.\par \par \uc2 He noted a recent drop in coffee production in Colombia, along with lower global prices for coffee beans, have put the iconic Colombian coffee farmer in dire straits.\par \par \uc2 "We know the problem--production is low and export prices aren't sufficient," Gomez said, adding a weaker peso would help in this regard. "So let's buy more dollars and see what happens."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gomez pulled no punches in his criticism of the bank's board, which is led by bank Chairman Jose Dario Uribe.\par \par \uc2 "This is what happens when you have people leading powerful institutions who haven't done real business work in the real world," he said, saying he wasn't referring to the chairman specifically but rather the board in general.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gomez said he personally believes the peso needs to weaken to COP2,000 a dollar. The peso closed Tuesday at COP1,803.05 to the dollar. It is 8% stronger this year, but little changed versus 12 months ago.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gomez said he expects coffee production of around eight million 60-kilogram bags this year. That is well below the 11 million bags Colombia produces in a good year.\par \par \uc2 President Juan Manuel Santos, meanwhile, said he "believes there is space for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy more dollars" to weaken the peso, which he said would help coffee farmers.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dan Molinski at Dan.Molinski@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 14-08-12 2045GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120814e88e0014d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
