\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  finmin wants more "aggressive"  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carlos Vargas   \par \uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2012\par 03:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, June 12 (Reuters) - Colombian Finance Minster Juan Carlos Echeverry said on Tuesday he favored more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem strong gains by the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Colombia has attracted record foreign investment since beating back leftist rebels over the last decade, which has fueled the economy but also put appreciation pressure on the peso, making exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 In a move to counter the strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board of directors, of which Echeverry is member, is buying at least $20 million daily until early November.\par \par \uc2 "I maintain it's necessary that in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  we have a more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with immediate sterilization," Echeverry told lawmakers during a debate on the peso.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate remains a worrying variable. I've asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board to look at what other similar economies are doing like Peru. We can learn from them if one can have controlled inflation and a more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso is one of the world's strongest gaining \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , firming about 8.5 percent so far this year although in the last month the rising trend has slowed due to worries over the crisis in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Latin America has absorbed huge amounts of money as investors look for higher yields in what some policymakers have called a global "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war".\par \par \uc2 Europe's debt crisis and China's economic slowdown pose new threats to Latin American economies and markets, and as the region begins to cool down, governments are doing what they can to protect themselves from a wave of cheap goods.\par \par \uc2 While the United States and many European nations struggle to shore up their fiscal accounts, Colombia's financial management, buoyant economy and security advances were rewarded last year with an investment grade from major rating agencies.\par \par \uc2 The Andean nation's economy is seen growing around 5 percent this year with full-year inflation between 3 percent and 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Carlos Vargas; Editing by Anthony Boadle)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | RNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120613e86d00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
