\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia Central Bank \highlight\uc2 's May  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Was $420 Million\b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2012\par 00:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA--The Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it had purchased $420 million in the spot market last month under its policy of buying at least $20 million a day to drain U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and build up international reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar purchases in the spot market are aimed at preventing excessive strength in the peso. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 9% stronger against the dollar in 2012, although for the month of May the peso weakened 4% due to worries about Europe's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 In all this year, the bank has bought $1.58 billion, and it has pledged to continue the daily purchases through at least Nov. 4.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used a similar strategy in 2011, when it purchased $3.72 billion until October, then ended its policy of daily purchases as fears over the European debt crisis caused the peso to weaken on its own, rendering such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso weakened slightly Tuesday to close at COP1,779 to the dollar, from COP1,777 a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 -Write to Dan Molinski at dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 12-06-12 2227GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120612e86c0012u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
