\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia Central Bank \highlight\uc2 's April  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Was $380M\b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2012\par 22:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--The Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday it had purchased $380 million in the spot market last month under its policy of buying at least $20 million a day to drain U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and build up international reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar purchases in the spot market are aimed at preventing excessive strength in the peso. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 9% stronger against the dollar in 2012, a move that's hurting Colombian exporters.\par \par \uc2 In all this year, the bank has bought $1.16 billion, and it pledged at its most recent monetary policy meeting April 30 to continue the daily purchases through at least Nov. 4.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used a similar strategy in 2011, when it purchased $3.72 billion until October, then ended its policy of daily purchases as fears over the European debt crisis caused the peso to weaken on its own, rendering such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso gained Monday to close at COP1,754.20 to the dollar, from COP1,757.55 a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 57-310-867-6542; dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 07-05-12 2049GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120507e857000zx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
