\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Expected To Hold Rates As Inflation Slows\b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2012\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Economists say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep rates at 5.25% at Monday meeting\par \par \uc2 --Lower inflation data, elevated global risks reduce need for rate hike\par \par \uc2 --Bank's board may announce more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop peso's gains\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Dan Molinski \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--Colombia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to leave interest rates on hold at its meeting Monday as recently lower inflation and somewhat elevated global risks put the bank in a wait-and-see mode.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All seven analysts surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires said they expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's board will vote to hold its benchmark rate at 5.25% at its monetary policy meeting. The bank also left rates on hold at last month's meeting, following nine rate hikes going back to early 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While economists unanimously agree the bank will leave rates on hold Monday, there's less agreement as to whether the bank may act to try to stem a strengthening peso that's 9% stronger this year and 0.6% stronger over the past week, at COP1,763 for $1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank already \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, buying $20 million daily to remove excess U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and some analysts say the bank may announce Monday it's increasing this amount.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the bank's board members, Juan Carlos Echeverry, who also is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister, has been urging more aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the peso, saying exporters of bananas, flowers and other products cannot be competitive abroad unless the peso weakens to at least COP1,800 for $1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Colombia's economy has performed well for several years in a row, and is likely to expand more than 5% in 2012, fueled by a record levels of foreign investment into its oil and coal sectors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid the strong growth, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased interest rates for much of the past year due to concerns that consumer spending was accelerating too quickly, which might cause inflation to spike. But board members have recently indicate they're confident the rate hikes already implemented will be enough for now to rein in spending habits and keep inflation comfortably within the bank's 2% to 4% target range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer price inflation for the 12 months through last month was 3.4% after March saw a lower-than-expected 0.12% inflation rate as food prices declined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic data out last week came in higher than forecast, with retail sales rising a sharp 9.4% in February, year-on-year, and industrial output climbing 4.5%. But Alejandro Reyes, at Bogota brokerage Ultrabursatiles, says the sharp gains were likely a result of February being a leap year this year, meaning the month had one additional day for shoppers to spend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reyes also said he believes the bank's seven-member board will take into account a slight uptick in investors' concerns about the global economy. Countries such as Spain and Italy are battling with debt problems similar to those seen in Greece.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a moderation in optimism," the analyst said. "And so I think the bank will choose the so-called wait-and-see approach."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 57-310-867-6542; dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 26-04-12 1552GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120426e84q001g9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
