\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Action Doubtful At Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 1,346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2012\par 15:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:12 (Dow Jones) Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets today, though the board's unlikely to act on interest rates or \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite plenty of market chatter on both issues. \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is among the strongest in the world, up 7% against USD year-to-date at COP1,813/USD. COP strength is partially due to expectations of interest rate hikes in 2012, which is fueling carry trades. Exporters want the bank to buy dollars to stem COP strength, but economists say such \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't happen until the peso gains to COP1,750 or so, and say rate hikes will wait til Feb, at least. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:12 (Dow Jones) Brazil and Argentina soon to announce new biodiesel usage mandates amid tightening energy supplies in growing South American economies, says Thomas Mielke, analyst with Oil World. Argentinian mandate for biodiesel content of country's diesel fuel seen rising to 10% within a week or two, with government and industry agreed on backing the rise, while Brazil's mandate seen rising to 7% in the near-term and 10% over the next one to two years, Mielke says, speaking at industry event in Florida. South American soybean exports likely to decline as a result, according to analyst. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:58 (Dow Jones) Brazilian independent driller OGX (OGXP3.BR, OGXPY) among the day's leading gainers on the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange after the company started its first oil production Saturday. OGX shares up 0.4% shortly after open, outperforming Ibovespa's 1.2% decline. OGX initiated procedure to open first well at the Waimea field, officially moving from a pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  company to oil producer. First production had originally been expected in the 3Q11. Output will ramp up slowly to about 20,000 barrels a day to start, growing to about 50,000 barrels as additional wells connected. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:56 (Dow Jones) Kellogg's (K) earnings are likely to disappoint during the next two years, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says, prompting a downgrade to neutral from overweight. And the firm sees problems beyond the well-known supply chain issues that have hampered the stock since its last earnings report. K's categories "may not be as attractive as perhaps believed," with three of K's top four categories -- cereal, crackers and cookies -- having grown slower than the broader food group as a whole in recent years. Firm also worries that cereal is about to become more promotional after RAH's Post cereals business said it plans to become more aggressive. K down 1.4% premarket to $49.03. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:55 (Dow Jones) Brigantine Advisors expects \uc2 Brooks Automation\uc2  (BRKS), which makes equipment used to make semiconductors, to guide for better revenue and earnings growth in F2Q when it reports F1Q results on Feb 9. Firm says BRKS still gathers the majority of its revenue from the semiconductor sector, while it is looking to diversify into life sciences, and should gain from rising capex budgets at Intel (INTC) and \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 . Brigantine raises recommendation to buy from hold and places a $13.50 price target on BRKS, which closed at $10.86 Friday. (patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:55 (Dow Jones) There's been plenty of worry this month about margins at \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2  (ANF), and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  weighs in this morning by contending F4Q discounts were "deeper and longer lasting" than expected. "Most surprisingly, ANF's promotions extended to its London flagship for the first time," the first time an international flagship has done so." \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cuts its EPS target for the quarter by 14% and lowers margin assumptions amid ANF going "50% off entire store for much of December." ANF, inactive premarket, is down 3.3% this month amid the broader market's rally. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:46 (Dow Jones) Down 1/3 since early July, auto-parts maker \uc2 Tenneco\uc2  (TEN) gets upgraded to outperform by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . "Investor sentiment is fairly negative and expectations are low" heading into Thursday's 4Q report and outlook update. "We acknowledge there is risk to Q4 and the consensus 2012 Ebitda. However, it appears to us investors have already braced for a sloppy Q4 and a recalibration of forecasts." Nonetheless, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  says, "We believe risk/reward is favorable at current levels." It also boosts its fair-value estimate by $6 to $37-41. TEN is up 1.6% premarket at $33. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:42 (Dow Jones) TrimTabs has gone to "cautiously bearish" on US stocks from neutral, citing waning demand and extreme bullish sentiment. TrimTabs' Demand Index, which tracks multiple flow and sentiment data, tumbled to 27 as of Thursday--a 28-month low--from 57.1 a week earlier. Below 50 is considered bearish. Separately, 45% of hedge-fund managers surveyed by TrimTabs/Barclay Hedge were bullish on the S&P 500, a 13-month high. Still, the only real positive sign for stocks, TrimTabs says, is that buyback announcements jumped to $22.8B in the latest week, the most in 9 months. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:42 (Dow Jones) The price index for personal consumption expenditures, a fancy name for a measure of inflation, increases 2.4% in the year ended in December. And the core rate -- which excludes food and energy -- is up 1.8% for the year, today's personal income report shows. Thanks to slack in the economy and high joblessness, price pressures remain virtually nil. The Fed breathes a sigh of relief and remains focused of supporting economic growth. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:40 (Dow Jones) Though the FTC is suing to block nursing-home pharmacy Omnicare (OCR) from buying rival PharMerica (PMC), that doesn't mean agency will also block \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  (ESRX) from buying Medco (MHS), Stifel argues. ESRX is awaiting clearance to buy pharmacy-benefit rival and create industry's biggest firm. "While some investors may be concerned about the FTC's approval the \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2 /Medco merger in light of the news" about OCR's plans, Stifel says, "we continue to believe the merger is likely to be approved." Firm notes combined OCR/PMC market share "would have been much higher" than the combined ESRX/MHS market share. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:39 (Dow Jones) A broad range of euro-zone-related anxieties is helping Treasurys extend that market's recent rally. Greece still hasn't penned an agreement with its private lenders while Germany's finance minister has warned it might deny the country more bailout cash. Meanwhile, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warns about Spain's worsening economy and Italy's so-so auctions are raising a bit less than the maximum targeted amount. Treasurys climbed steadily overnight and were recently around session highs; the 10-year is up 1/2, pushing the yield down to 1.840%. In contrast, Portugal's 5-year bond yield just hit a record high of 22.01%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:37 (Dow Jones) US stocks have been boosted by a "favorable seasonal backdrop and improving economic data," \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  notes, but "as the November-January seasonal boost fades into February, and economic expectations become harder to beat, the path for stocks may get significantly more difficult to navigate in coming weeks." Firm thinks stocks are due for a "near-term correction," and corporate earnings "will offer little comfort in the near term, in our view." \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  thinks "2012 may be a year in which investors are persistently weighing the give and take of rolling economic surprises and halting earnings momentum, thereby resulting in fairly inconsistent price trends." (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-30-12 0912ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120130e81u0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
