\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Colombia restricts dollar deals to halt volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2011\par 20:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment from finance minister, background)\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Colombia on Wednesday imposed a limit on the amount some pension funds can invest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, seeking to reduce volatility and speculation, the Finance Ministry said.\par \par \uc2 The government action follows a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledge on Sept. 30 to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility when \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso <COP2=STFX> moves 2 percent in either direction.\par \par \uc2 "The limit seeks to restrict trades whose intent is to make short-term bets, but not to restrict the execution of a stable investment strategy," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's "obligated" pension funds -- those that receive contributions from the amount workers are required to pay -- cannot invest more than 2.5 percent of their holdings in the spot or forward markets over any five-day period.\par \par \uc2 Those pension funds hold assets worth a combined total of $52 billion, limiting their investment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to no more than $1.3 billion over any five-day period, the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 All Colombians must pay 4 percent of their salaries into pension funds. There is a system for those who wish to save more, but those "voluntary" funds are not subject to the new rules.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry said the government noticed some unusual trends in trades made by pension funds that hold the retirement savings of ordinary Colombians, noting that they sold a net $880 million worth of U.S. dollars from May to June but bought a net $1.12 billion from August to September.\par \par \uc2 "Variations of nearly a billion dollars in the space of two or three months ... yes, I think you can categorize that as speculation," Echeverry told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso turned negative versus the dollar this year on Sept. 12, losing another 5.9 percent since then, putting the brakes on a strengthening trend that began in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in September it would call auctions in the spot market for an amount of $200 million when the market rate deviates 2 percent or more from its 10-day moving average. But it has not had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  since making the announcement.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed 0.14 percent stronger on Wednesday at 1,900.3 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nelson Bocanegra; Writing by by Daniel Trotta; Editing by Leslie Adler and Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-PENSION (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111022e7aj000jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
