\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Colombia Content With Peso's Plunge, In No Hurry To Halt It\b0\par\par\uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 18:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --Peso has dropped 10% since Aug. 31 amid worries of global recession \par --\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  waiting two weeks before any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par --Finance Minister Echeverry 'not unhappy' with peso's decline \par    By Dan Molinski \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--Colombia's peso has plunged 10% in recent weeks but authorities say they won't consider \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until at least the middle of October, a signal that the government is content if not outright giddy that its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is finally falling against the perennially weak dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The peso was recently trading at COP1,979 for $1, which is 10% weaker than the COP1,778 seen at the end of August, as fears of a global recession cause investors to unwind their bets on riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 8% weaker over the past two weeks and 3% weaker over just the past two days. Such volatile moves for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could sometimes lead to high inflation and a host of other problems, and thus would cause many central banks to sound the alarm bells and immediately start \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market under control.\par \par \uc2 Not \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however. Though it unveiled a powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan Friday in which it will step in and buy or sell $200 million dollars if the peso bounces more than 2% up or down from its 10-day average, the bank said Monday that the program won't take effect until Oct. 14.\par \par \uc2 That delay, say analysts, shows that while central bankers in Bogota are keenly aware of the risks to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, they also know such volatility can be a good thing if it brings the peso to levels they have long been aiming for.\par \par \uc2 Government officials have said they are comfortable with the peso in the COP1,900s range, while Colombia's national industry association, known as ANDI, says a level around COP2,000 would be good.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso reached its strongest level of the year in July, at COP1,747 to the dollar, an exchange rate nearly 50% stronger than February 2009, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was around COP2,600 per dollar. Such strength has significantly damaged Colombia's vital export sector, as many of their products have become prohibitively expensive abroad.\par \par \uc2 "The current levels around 1,960 isn't a level where you could say that the peso is actually weak. We've been there many times," said Felipe Hernandez at RBS Securities. "The peso is finally weaker and that's what the government wants. Exporters and everyone feels comfortable."\par \par \uc2 For years, the knee-jerk reaction by authorities in Latin America to a falling local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stop the bleeding. After all, a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often created double-digit or triple-digit inflation rates, as already-expensive imported goods suddenly became exorbitantly expensive.\par \par \uc2 But in Colombia and many other Latin American countries, the years-long trend toward a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilized prices for refrigerators, cars, computers and other so-called tradable goods. Flat-screen televisions in Colombia have even seen a sharp decline in prices, partly because of technological advances but also due to a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's why tradable goods here have seen inflation rates near zero over the past year, a trend that's helped offset the rise in local goods such as food and transportation and has kept overall annual inflation within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% to 4% range.\par \par \uc2 As such, when Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry was asked last week what he thought about the peso's sharp decline, he said he was fine with it.\par \par \uc2 "It's good for exporters, with people that compete with imports, so I'm not unhappy with what has happened," said Echeverry, who is also a board member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Colombia's central bankers, of course, must remain vigilant of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. Many local companies have a large amount of dollar-denominated debts but earn their income in pesos, so a weaker peso could make it harder to repay those debts. But government officials say most local companies hedge their dollar-denominated debts.\par \par \uc2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 57-310-867-6542; dan.molinski@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 04, 2011 12:48 ET (16:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111004e7a400595\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
