\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Adds To  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 19:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1:57 (Dow Jones) With markets moving fast and furious, it seems even central bankers can be remiss. Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday that to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, it will auction $200M when the weakening peso's exchange rate veers 2% from its 10-day average. Problem is, that 2% would have been triggered immediately today and probably each day this week. So the bank sent an addendum Monday, saying auctions won't be able to be triggered until Oct. 14. But that also means two more weeks without any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  help. The peso is down 1% today at COP1,950/dollar. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:53 (Dow Jones) Oil-drilling firms Patterson (PTEN) and \uc2 Helmerich & Payne\uc2  (HP) are slumping as Bernstein downgrades them to market-perform and cuts their price targets some 22% to $30 and $57, respectively. "The downside risk to customer spending suggests that the risk/reward on the land drillers is less favorable versus peers," prompting Bernstein's downgrades. It adds, "While H&P and Patterson are undervalued on an absolute basis, in our view, the downside risk to North American capex and downside to trough book-value multiples points to superior risk/reward" for oil-services firms and offshore drillers. PTEN is down 7.6% to $16.02 and HP falls 6% to $38.17. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:44 (Dow Jones) 30-year yield continues to sink as prices get driven up by the Federal Reserve's \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist purchase this afternoon and the general flight among investors toward safer assets. The 30-year bond now up 3 1/32 in price as yields sink 13.6bps to 2.784%--that's the lowest yield since early Jan. 2009, in the midst of global financial crisis sparked by \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ' collapse. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:41 (Dow Jones) Investors aren't sharing the optimism of Cemex (CX CEMEX.MX) officials that the global building materials company will comply with its debt covenants this year and next, continuing to push local shares down to levels not seen in over a decade. Cemex CPO shares recently off 11% to MXN3.95. Increased cash flow, or asset sales, could help CX meet its leverage goals and be a short-term catalyst for the stock, Banorte-Ixe says, but not enough to substantially change a complicated medium-term outlook. In NY, CX down 16% at $2.66. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:41 (Dow Jones) Toyota (TM) may be saying its Japanese production is at full strength for the first time since March's natural disasters, but that's not helping US sales yet. What has been the world's best-selling auto maker the past several years has now trailed one-time also-ran Chrysler in America in 4 of the past 5 months. TM expects US sales to finally start exceeding prior-year levels starting this month--they were down double-digits again in September. But after last year's mess following the recall and the lost output in 2011 after the earthquake and tsunami, has TM permanently lost some of its US market share as a result? (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:32 (Dow Jones) The parent of American Airlines was briefly valued at less than $600M following a share-price collapse that has seen AMR repeatedly trigger circuit-breakers. Speculation about a potential filing for bankruptcy protection--something the company maintains it wants to avoid--comes on word of a large number of pilot retirements as staff try to capture pension payouts at a higher stock price. AMR has been canny in raising cash in recent months, but its stock-market value trails the $700M-plus it wants to raise in a bond sale. The slide to an 8 1/2-year low also threatens to complicate efforts to spin off AMR's Eagle commuter arm. Stock is down 29% at $2.09. (doug.cameron@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:29 (Dow Jones) Trading volumes for online brokerages improved in recent weeks, but still plunged from August to September as firms including \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2  (AMTD), \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2  (SCHW) and E*Trade (ETFC) faced difficult month-to-month comparisons, Sandler O'Neill says, citing its own research. While September usually benefits from a pickup in activity following the typical summer slowdown, trading appeared weaker due to the surge in volatility in August. Firm projects September daily average revenue trade declines of 20% for AMTD, SCHW, ETFC, and Interactive Brokers' (IBKR) cleared trades. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:15 (Dow Jones) Long bonds lead Treasury prices to session highs, as stocks hit new lows on the day. The general risk aversion is fueling this all-too-familiar selling of equities and buying of safer Treasurys. But the notable winner continues to be the long bond, benefiting from the Fed's debut purchase of 25- to 30-year bonds this afternoon as part \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist. The 30-year bond now up 2 20/32 in price as yields sink 11.8bps to 2.802%. Benchmark 10-year notes up 31/32 to yield 1.821%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:12 (Dow Jones) S&P 500 has slipped below the Sept 22 low of 1114.22, leaving little in terms of technical significance until Aug 9 intraday low of 1102. That also happens to mark the 38.2% retracement of the bull market rally off the March 2009 low (667) to the April 2011 high (1371). Below that, the next major downside level to watch would be 1010-1020 area, which includes the 50% retracement of the bull market and the July 2010 low. S&P 500 down 16 at 1115, off an intraday low of 1113.59. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:11 (Dow Jones) The latest downdraft for US stocks looks more technical in nature than anything fundamental driving the action. The S&P 500 falls below 1120, considered a key support level the past few months as it represents September's lows. "This is a technical trade that's taking over," says Ryan Larson at RBC. Better-than-expected ISM manufacturing data this morning is being overshadowed by continued worries related to Greece and Europe's ongoing debt crisis. "People continue to use any rally we get to take profits and exit positions," Larson says. "Until that changes, it will be hard to sustain any meaningful move higher." S&P 500 down 1.5% at 1115 while the Dow industrials shed 131. (steven.russolillio@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:09 (Dow Jones) Good news for Detroit: Trucks sales are rolling. Trucks like the Ford's (F) F-150 make big money for the domestic auto makers and in September, sales soared. Ford sold 54,410 F-Series trucks, a huge number, up 14.7%. Sales of GM's Chevy Silverado jumped 36% to 43,698; GMC Sierra was up 25.5% at 13,904. Chrysler sold 24,572 Dodge Rams, up 45%. Trucks sales are helped by seasonal factors. A lot of commercial buyers like contractors buy in the fall. And incentives were higher. Ford was heavily promoting deals offering an extra $1,000 on trade-ins; GM was offering 0% loans. It doesn't hurt either that gas prices were down. (neal.boudette@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:04 (Dow Jones) MGM, which didn't fall as much as fellow casino operators WYNN and \uc2 Las Vegas Sands\uc2  (LVS) last week, is catching up today as the stock hits a 2-year low as worries about growth in Macau continue to pressure the sector. Nomura today notes, "Slow down in China? This is not a new topic--the debate is whether it'll be a hard landing or a soft landing." Meanwhile, "Without more attractive investment alternatives, we do not see why private equity investors would stop investing in junkets." There hasn't yet been a notable slowdown in Macau gambling-revenue growth, but investors aren't showing signs of waiting for one before selling. MGM is down 6.8% at $8.66 while WYNN falls 3% and LVS drops 1.7%. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-03-11 1357ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111003e7a3000k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
