\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Colombia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Kimball   \par \uc2 1,039 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2011\par 16:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Sept 2 (Reuters) - Election violence, on-going bloodshed caused by guerrillas and drug gangs, and sensitive ties with neighbors Venezuela and Ecuador as well as the United States are key risks to watch in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 ELECTIONS, VICTIMS AND SCANDALS\par \par \uc2 Rising violence ahead of local elections in October as armed groups jockey for control of local councils, mayoralties and governorships, and therefore control over resources and drug routes, is worrying the government. [ID:nN1E76O0VB]\par \par \uc2 The independent Electoral Observation Mission says about a fifth of the approximately 1,000 municipalities are at risk of violence or fraud. President Juan Manuel Santos' still-high popularity in opinion polls has taken a hit over perceptions of increased corruption and insecurity as well as his government's response to heavy downpours that battered the Andean nation.\par \par \uc2 Defense Minister Rodrigo Rivera resigned in late August for failing to stem attacks from Marxist guerrillas.\par \par \uc2 A "victims law" that will pay reparations to those affected by decades of armed conflict and return land to displaced peasants is aimed at pacifying rural areas. But some experts fear a violent reaction from former paramilitary groups and drug barons who took over that land. [ID:nN25183324]\par \par \uc2 Gunmen have killed more than a dozen community leaders pushing for land restitution in areas where outlawed militias, cocaine lords and rebels still fight for control of the drug trade. [ID:nN24146586] [ID:nN08227877]\par \par \uc2 Colombia's improved security and fiscal performance were cited by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  for giving the country back investment grade status [ID:nN31302945] and the passage of recent reforms led to an upgrade by Fitch. [ID:nN1E75L1JH] [ID:nN08261853] [ID:nN09254206] [ID:nN14198067]\par \par \uc2 Santos has a majority in Congress and recently received support from the Green Party, bringing his backing in the legislature to more than 90 percent -- a number only matched during the National Front period between 1958 and 1974 that followed more than a decade of rural violence. That is likely to ensure his reform agenda in the second half of 2011 will go smoothly.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- More attacks against victims' rights workers.\par \par \uc2 -- An increase in violence as elections draw near.\par \par \uc2 LEFTIST REBELS, DRUGS AND NEW GANGS\par \par \uc2 Santos is keeping up former President Alvaro Uribe's tough stance against left-wing rebels, ex-paramilitary gangs and cocaine traffickers. He has vowed to break up gangs linked to ex-paramilitaries, crack down on drug trafficking and improve security in the next three years. [ID:nN24118982] His government almost captured the leader of Colombia's largest guerrilla group, the FARC, earlier this year. [ID:nN1E762085]\par \par \uc2 The FARC, Latin America's oldest insurgency, says it wants talks. But Santos, a former defense minister, says the rebels must end hostilities, halt criminal activity and release hostages before any negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Colombian rebels, however, have answered that with a series of bombings, murders and kidnappings across the Andean nation.[ID:nN1E7680AK] [ID:nN1E76906A] They have also attacked energy and mining infrastructure, although the level of bombings is far below what was registered a decade before. [ID:nN1E76D0RU] [ID:nN14198897] [ID:nN04120440]\par \par \uc2 Attacks against energy infrastructure rose 18 percent in January to July this year versus 2010, and a recent spate of kidnappings and attacks against oil workers has forced the government to say it would study new ways to provide more security to oil companies. [ID:nN1E772186] [ID:nN1E7760BJ]\par \par \uc2 Colombia remains the world's No. 1 cocaine producer, and multiple illegal armed groups are engaged in the drug trade. Groups have fragmented and rebels have formed alliances with drug-trafficking gangs and ex-paramilitaries in some areas and are battling for control of others. Cocaine output, however, dropped last year due to heavy rains, crop eradication and military enforcement, according to government estimates.\par \par \uc2 New criminal bands known by their Spanish acronym "Bacrim" are widely seen as the major emerging threat. They are linked to former paramilitaries and drove a rise in massacres in 2010, the United Nations says. [ID:nN24266002] The October elections will be a test of what role the groups may play in politics.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Strikes on FARC leadership, large-scale desertions.\par \par \uc2 -- More bombings of oil, coal infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 -- Increased sway of new criminal gangs.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 , RATES AND THE ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 With investment grade ratings from three credit agencies now under Colombia's belt and robust economic growth, authorities are keeping a watch on inflation and the peso's appreciation as investment flows increase. [ID:nN1E76B127] [ID:nN1E76715F]\par \par \uc2 Greater global economic uncertainty was cited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its reason to keep interest rates steady at its August meeting after six straight hikes, and the strong peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a major worry for policy makers. [ID:nN1E77I0ZA]\par \par \uc2 Santos said Latin America would better cope with global financial turbulence if its central banks were to coordinate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN1E77F1TJ]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces more pressure to pay for repairs to infrastructure after torrential rains destroyed roads and flooded towns, killing some 400 people and disrupting the lives of 3 million people. The government estimates damages in the billions of dollars. That could fuel inflation and hit economic growth, now forecast by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at between 4.5 percent and 6.5 percent for this year.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- More pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates stable.\par \par \uc2 -- New fiscal measures to fund reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 TENSIONS WITH NEIGHBORS, U.S. TRADE DEAL\par \par \uc2 Ties with the United States, Colombia's main trade partner and for long a close ally, are strained over a stalled free trade deal and the recent expiration of trade benefits.\par \par \uc2 Colombia has proposed measures to improve labor relations with its unions, who have suffered violent attacks in the past, in an effort to overcome resistance to the trade deal in the U.S. Congress. [ID:nN1E77E098]\par \par \uc2 Tensions between Colombia and left-wing neighbors Venezuela and Ecuador have eased considerably since Santos took office last year, but ideological differences are always just below the surface.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- How Santos manages any flare-ups in relations again.\par \par \uc2 -- Improving bilateral trade with Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 -- More moves to approve U.S. free trade pact.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Daniel Wallis and Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RISKS/   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110902e792000v2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
