\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia Peso Stable Despite Worries Of Excessive Strength\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2011\par 19:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --Colombia peso in tight-range trading over past 3 weeks \par --\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  just 2% stronger versus dollar since August 2010 \par --Banks buying dollars at COP1,780 due to global jitters \par    By Dan Molinski \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--Colombia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could remain bound to the tight trading ranges it has seen over the past three weeks, pleasing monetary authorities who have been working to prevent excessive strength against the weak dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Despite persistent concerns that the peso is on the verge of strong gains that could spiral out of control and cause serious damage to Colombia's important export sector, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a mere 2% stronger compared with 12 months ago.\par \par \uc2 During late-morning trading Tuesday in Bogota, a dollar was fetching COP1,783, making the peso 0.2% stronger on the day. Since Aug. 10, the peso's closing level has hovered in a narrow range between COP1,766 and COP1,796.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say a combination of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and recent volatility in global markets, which reduces the peso's appeal, is likely to keep any would-be peso strength muffled for the next several weeks, if not months.\par \par \uc2 "This lateral trend we've been seeing is likely to continue," said Juan Pablo Viera, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Colombia's biggest brokerage InterBolsa, in Medellin. "The range for the dollar, give or take a few, is between COP1,770 and COP1,800."\par \par \uc2 Viera said his brokerage has recently been seeing "important buyers of dollars" each time the peso strengthens toward the COP1,780 mark.\par \par \uc2 The buyers, he said, are mostly traders working for Colombian banks and other financial institutions. "It's because of the volatility in global markets. They cover their positions by buying U.S. Treasurys and things like that."\par \par \uc2 That's not to say there's no risk the peso could make a strong move upward, especially when and if global markets settle down. Colombia has been experiencing a huge increase in foreign investment into its oil and coal sectors, which has flooded the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with dollars and kept constant pressure on the peso to strengthen.\par \par \uc2 But analysts say the government's dollar-buying efforts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should continue to help quell any bursts of peso strength from foreign investment flows.\par \par \uc2 Authorities said in April they were creating a $1.2 billion fund from dollars bought locally as a way to rein in the peso's gains. Additionally, Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys at least $20 million daily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 In a report Tuesday, RBS analysts said the government has plenty of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with which to continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RBS noted that Colombian tax authorities brought in COP47 trillion ($26 billion) during the first six months of the year, a 33% rise year over year. Colombia's tax haul for the full year 2011 is expected to easily surpass government targets.\par \par \uc2 The increased tax revenue, RBS said, "reflect the strong cash position of the government in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and imply it does not need to sell dollars this year and could even use some of the excess cash to buy dollars."\par \par \uc2 -By Dan Molinski, Dow Jones Newswires; 57-310-867-6542; dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 30-08-11 1704GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110830e78u0014h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
