\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Colombia cbank pauses rate hikes on global worries\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 20:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Cenbank board decision to hold rates was unanimous\par \par \uc2 * Move seen as pause in tightening, not end of cycle\par \par \uc2 * Monetary body did not mention any new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures\par \par \uc2 * Board would like to see weaker peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds analyst comment, details)\par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra and Eduardo Garcia\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paused its tightening cycle Friday after six straight months of interest rate hikes, citing concerns over turbulence in global financial markets and its possible impact on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The bank held its benchmark interest rate steady at 4.50 percent in a unanimous decision after dissenting votes at its past two policy meetings.\par \par \uc2 The decision caught some analysts off guard after the bank raised growth forecasts for Colombia last month.\par \par \uc2 Economists saw it as a temporary pause to gauge the effects of recent market turmoil. Many analysts expect the bank to resume rate increases in order to keep a lid on rising inflation in Latin America's fifth largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "They're talking about a pause, a pause is not a completion of the hikes, I believe that what they're doing is taking a breath and then they'll continue (with the increases)," said Andres Pardo, head of economic research at Corficolombiana.\par \par \uc2 PRUDENT TO HALT\par \par \uc2 Colombia was one of the last countries in Latin America to start upping rates and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has faced pressure from the government to hold borrowing costs steady, as Chile and Peru have done.\par \par \uc2 Still, the bank struck a pessimistic tone in its statement, saying inflation was rising above the mid-point of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2 percent to 4 percent target.\par \par \uc2 Nineteen of 35 economists polled by Reuters this week expected the bank to raise rates 25 basis points. The remaining 16 said the bank would hold rates.\par \par \uc2 As fears grow the developed world is shifting from slow growth to stagnation, the bank said it was prudent to see what effect market turbulence might have on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 "The increased uncertainty in international financial markets and its possible effect on the growth of the global economy, that was a key element," Jose Dario Uribe, general manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters, following the policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 From the United States and Italy to Japan and Greece, slow growth is hobbling the capacity of governments to service their debts, spooking markets and sapping confidence and so further darkening the economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 To many economists, high-growth emerging markets like Colombia appear better placed to weather the storm.\par \par \uc2 NO NEW \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  MEASURES\par \par \uc2 Colombia has grappled with the dilemma of whether to raise rates to combat inflation. Higher rates have made their assets more attractive, stoked their domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and made exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 Chile, Peru, Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to tame their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but are struggling given a weak dollar abroad and high interest rate differentials with the United States which attract capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not mention on Friday any new measures to combat the strength of the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has firmed 7.2 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The bank's current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of purchasing a minimum of $20 million daily is set to expire on September 30 -- the date of the board's next policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 "Definitely (the exchange rate) does not leave us calm (at the current level), and as we've said, I would say, that all members of the bank's board would like to have a more depreciated exchange rate," Uribe said.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Jack Kimball; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RATES/ (UPDATE 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110822e78j00161\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
