\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Colombia holds rates due to global market worries\b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 20:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Aug 19 (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its benchmark interest rate at 4.5 percent on Friday after six straight increases, citing uncertainties in global markets.\par \par \uc2 Analysts had been divided over whether the bank would hike its key interest rate or keep it steady, a Reuters poll of experts showed earlier this week. [ID:nN1E77G13Y]\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was one of the last in Latin America to start upping rates. It has faced pressure from the government to stop tightening monetary policy, as Chile and Peru have done. [ID:nN1E77H0WW] [ID:nN1E77A21B]\par \par \uc2 Many emerging markets have been grappling with the dilemma of whether to raise rates to combat inflation and risk stoking strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chile, Peru, Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to tame their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but are struggling given a weak dollar abroad and high interest rate differentials with the United States which attract capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Bogota Newsroom; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RATES/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110822e78j00160\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
