\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  seen maintaining firm trend in 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 17:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Peso strength may be offset by acquisitions near yr-end\par \par \uc2 * Experts see peso between 1,700 and 1,840 for rest of yr\par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Colombia's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep its firming trend due to expansive monetary policy in the United States and Europe, but its appreciation should be stymied by local firms' foreign acquisitions.\par \par \uc2 With debt woes weighing down Europe and the United States, investors have snapped up emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in regions like Latin America despite measures ranging from capital controls to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Experts see \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso <COP=STFX> trading between the 1,700 level -- seen as the government's pain threshold -- and 1,840, which would represent a 4 percent rise from the point at which it closed in end 2010.\par \par \uc2 "The flows continue to support the (upward) pressure on the peso," said Camilo Perez, director of economic research at \uc2 Banco de Bogota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While interest rates remain low in developed economies, Colombia like other emerging markets is hiking rates, which creates attractive differentials for investors and could bring in more dollars and more appreciation to their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wall Street economists see odds of around one-in-three the United States will slip back into recession, heightening expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve will launch another round of unconventional credit easing. [ID:nN1E778212]\par \par \uc2 That could unleash another flood of dollars into economies such as Colombia that have been battling appreciation pressures on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since U.S. monetary easing first began.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's attractiveness for outside investors has risen this year after three rating agencies gave the Andean country back its investment grade rating, Congress approved a raft of fiscal reforms and the economy continued growing strong.\par \par \uc2 The peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been on a firming trend since 2003 thanks to improved security opening up the nation as never before to foreign investment, but it has seen the strongest appreciation since October 2008, firming more than 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 Since mid-2009, Colombia's 200-day moving average has been on a firming trend, which has also held more or less true for its short-term moving averages.\par \par \uc2 ACQUISITION IMPACT\par \par \uc2 Taking the edge off the peso's strength will be a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying program of at least $20 million a day, a $1.2 billion fund abroad by the government and strong imports.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also expect dollar purchases from GrupoSura at the end of the year to fund its acquisition of some of ING's Latin American assets for around $3.8 billion -- which would cause short-term depreciation. [ID:nLDE76O03R]\par \par \uc2 "The GrupoSura purchase is very large, if they have to go out and buy a large amount of dollars, there is no cashflow in the market that can take it," said a director of a foreign desk at a bank operating in the country.\par \par \uc2 "But that would be a blip, then it would resume the natural inertia of the peso because what you see is clients who are selling dollars and those outside seem to have a mad desire to invest in Colombia," he said.\par \par \uc2 EXPORTS, IMPORTS\par \par \uc2 Coupled with portfolio inflows, Latin America's No. 4 oil producer expects $12 billion in foreign direct investment, mainly into oil and mining, this year -- experts say there's about $4.5 billion remaining to come into the country.\par \par \uc2 High commodity prices for Colombia's coal, oil and coffee will also help push up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "While people feel that commodity prices remain high and there are good terms of trade, clearly there are factors that suggest there is not a tendency to depreciate," said Daniel Nino, research manager at Bancolombia.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Jack Kimball; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110811e78b001i8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
