\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Colombia's Echeverry: Ready To Take Steps Against 'Excessive' Peso Gain\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2011\par 01:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more comments from the minister throughout, and comments on economic growth.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Matthew Cowley \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Colombia's government is "always ready" to take steps to stem any "excessive" appreciation of the peso caused by a "temporary, unsustainable" flood of money from advanced countries, the government's top finance official said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not fighting the trend. I'm fighting against the temporary excessive appreciation," Finance Minister \uc2 Juan Carlos Echeverry\uc2  told Dow Jones Newswires in a broad interview.\par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to anticipate. But you have to be there, the market needs to know you're present."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September 2008, buying at least $20 million per day. Echeverry said this has been "very efficient," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Still, since early April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has once again started to gain, prompting some calls for the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry said \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to avoid capital controls as much as possible, as they have been shown not to work and can introduce distortions. But the monetary policies adopted by advanced economies, including low interest rates and so-called quantitative easing, have led to a wall of cash flooding into a number of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "The question of capital controls depends on your pain threshold. And you never know what the threshold is until you're there," he said.\par \par \uc2 Colombia has been putting its fiscal house in order, which allows the government more ability to maneuver in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, he said.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, having the government books in order makes it easier to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely, Echeverry said. On the other hand, it also helps in these extraordinary times when the government takes steps, he said.\par \par \uc2 The government has introduced legislation to fix pensions, health care, royalties from mining and oil, which will improve the country's fiscal position.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the government saved $1.5 billion in dividends from the government-run oil company, \uc2 Ecopetrol SA\uc2  (EC, ECOPETROL.BO, ECP.T). Tax revenue is up sharply this year, and the government plans to save some of that money as well, Echeverry said, without saying how much would be set aside.\par \par \uc2 The government also plans to use that tax revenue to pay off about 10 trillion pesos (around $5.5 billion) in securities known as TES coming due in May, Echeverry said. It has brought forward tax deadlines from the second half of the year so as to have the money, he said.\par \par \uc2 The government's borrowing plan for the rest of the year totals some $1 billion to $1.5 billion from international markets, the minister said, adding that he doesn't expect to borrow more than that. The government is waiting until the fiscal legislation is passed by Congress, probably in June, to be able to have a better picture to present to investors, he said.\par \par \uc2 Standard and Poor's raised Colombia's sovereign credit ratings to investment-grade last month, and Echeverry said he will meet with the other two major agencies, Moody's Investors Service and Fitch Ratings, on Monday. Both still have Colombia in speculative territory.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry is expecting gross domestic product to expand by 5.5% to 6% this year, with most sectors of the economy, including oil, trade and manufacturing all showing robust expansion. Only agriculture and the financial system are lagging at the moment, he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the government likely won't need more approvals from the legislature to spend on reconstruction efforts in parts of the country damaged by rain at the end of 2010, Echeverry said.\par \par \uc2 "We have already executed a large part of the money" for reconstruction, the minister said. "It's possible that we won't have to go to Congress to execute the resources, and that we won't have to resort to a budget amendment."\par \par \uc2 "I think we will have the resources needed to be able to cope with the winter without additional legislation," Echeverry said.\par \par \uc2 The minister said that a recent decision by Canada's \uc2 Greystar Resources Ltd\uc2 . (GYSLF, GLS.T) to withdraw an environmental licensing for its Angostura gold mine project in northeastern Colombia shouldn't deter mining investments.\par \par \uc2 "We need resources but not at the expense of others. There's a tradeoff," Echeverry said. "What's important in each case is to quickly tell investors whether you can or can't, or whether you can under certain conditions, but that's the same in Canada, Australia, or wherever."\par \par \uc2 Greystar ended its years-long bid to mine for gold in the highlands of Colombia following mounting opposition to the plan from environmentalists and business leaders. The company has said it will study to see whether the project is still viable.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 16, 2011 19:55 ET (23:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110416e74g000qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
