\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Colombia Finance Minister: Always Considering New  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Steps\b0\par\par\uc2 1,158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2011\par 21:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:14 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Focus Is Short-Term Appreciation Of Peso\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:14 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Aim To Save More Tax Revenue This Year\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:15 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: May Not Need Congress For Reconstruction Funds\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:15 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Focus Is Short-Term Appreciation Of Peso\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:15 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Aim To Save More Tax Revenue This Year\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:16 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: May Not Need Congress For Reconstruction Funds\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:16 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Will Pay May Bond Redemptions With Taxes\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:17 EDT *DJ  Colombia's Echeverry: Will Pay May Bond Redemptions With Taxes\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 15:59 EDT DJ  Colombia Echeverry: Ready To Take Steps Against Any 'Excessive' Peso Gain\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Colombia's government is "always ready" to take steps to stem any "excessive" appreciation of the peso caused by a "temporary, unsustainable" flood of money from advanced countries, the government's top finance official said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not fighting the trend. I'm fighting against the temporary excessive appreciation," Finance Minister \uc2 Juan Carlos Echeverry\uc2  told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "It's difficult to anticipate. But you have to be there, the market needs to know you're present."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September 2008, buying at least $20 million per day. Echeverry said this has been "very efficient," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Still, since early April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has once again started to gain, prompting some calls for the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry said \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to avoid capital controls as much as possible, as they have been shown not to work and can introduce distortions. But the monetary policies adopted by advanced economies, including low interest rates and so-called quantitative easing, have led to a wall of cash flooding into a number of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "The question of capital controls depends on your pain threshold. And you never know what the threshold is until you're there," he said.\par \par \uc2 Colombia has been putting its fiscal house in order, which allows the government more ability to maneuver in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, he said. The government has introduced legislation to fix pensions, health care, royalties from mining and oil, which will improve the country's fiscal position.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the government saved $1.5 billion in dividends from the government-run oil company, \uc2 Ecopetrol SA\uc2  (EC, ECOPETROL.BO, ECP.T).\par \par \uc2 Tax revenue is up sharply this year, and the government plans to save some of that money as well, Echeverry said, without saying how much would be set aside.\par \par \uc2 The government also plans to use that tax revenue to pay off about 10 trillion pesos (around $5.5 billion) in securities known as TES coming due in May, Echeverry said. It has brought forward tax deadlines from the second half of the year so as to have the money, he said.\par \par \uc2 The government's borrowing plan for the rest of the year totals some $1 billion to $1.5 billion from international markets, the minister said, adding that he doesn't expect to borrow more than that. The government is waiting until the fiscal legislation is passed by Congress, probably in June, to be able to have a better picture to present to investors, he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the government likely won't need more approvals from the legislature to spend on reconstruction efforts in parts of the country damaged by rain at the end of 2010, Echeverry said.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 16 Apr 2011 16:00 EDT DJ  Colombia Echeverry: Ready To Take Steps Against Any 'Excessive' Peso Gain\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Colombia's government is "always ready" to take steps to stem any "excessive" appreciation of the peso caused by a "temporary, unsustainable" flood of money from advanced countries, the government's top finance official said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not fighting the trend. I'm fighting against the temporary excessive appreciation," Finance Minister \uc2 Juan Carlos Echeverry\uc2  told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "It's difficult to anticipate. But you have to be there, the market needs to know you're present."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September 2008, buying at least $20 million per day. Echeverry said this has been "very efficient," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Still, since early April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has once again started to gain, prompting some calls for the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry said \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to avoid capital controls as much as possible, as they have bee