\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Market Debt,  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  End February Little Moved\b0\par\par\uc2 931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2011\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Emerging-market debt ends February with little movement\par \par \uc2 -Chinese commitment to yuan's slow appreciation allowed minor gains by Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -Watch for key rate decisions from Brazil and Poland this week\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Cynthia Lin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market assets ended February on an ambivalent note as the lingering unrest across the Middle East held \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and credit of the developing world within a tight band, ahead of a data-heavy week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk premium on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index global remained flat for much of the day, but ended two basis points wider, to 303 basis points over Treasurys. Its index gained nearly 0.1% in price terms on Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Frankly there hasn't been any big drivers in terms of EM debt," said John Peta, portfolio manager at Acadian Asset Management, but "inflation is clearly a theme for the coming year. People in emerging-market worlds are more impacted by inflation so leaders in these countries have to be attuned to the higher inflation, and hiking rates is one way to put a dent in that...and perhaps they will let their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate more than people might expect."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last Friday, Russian and Colombian policymakers' decisions to hike rates came at somewhat of a surprise, Peta added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Grabbing much attention over the weekend, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao said in a public forum that China's 5-year growth target would be lowered to 7.0% from 7.5% for 2011-2015, marking the nation's shift away from speedy growth and toward a steadier, more value-driven role in production and domestic consumption. The Premier also reiterated his concerns about inflation, supporting the case for the yuan's gradual appreciation and further rate hikes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In India, Finance Minister Mukherjee presented the nation's annual budget, forecast to come in at a 5.1% deficit for 2011 and pared down to 4.6% in 2012. Amid the government's attempts to slim the deficit, the budget also included a hefty increase in spending over the next year, which will likely keep demand pressures strong, leaving the battle against inflation in the hands of its monetary authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  straddled the breakeven point on Monday, with Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gaining slightly against the U.S. dollar while its Latin American and Eastern European counterparts came in mixed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The news out of China and India set the stage for broad, but minor, gains by Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Indian rupee ended nearly 0.3% higher against the U.S. dollar, while the Korean won and Indonesia rupiah each gained more than 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Embig, Asia's regional index tipped one basis point tighter, but lost 0.11% in price terms. The notable leader was Vietnam, whose spread tighten 21 basis points, to 351 basis points over benchmark, gaining 1.21% on the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Eastern Europe, the Turkish lira was slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar, being one of the more oil-import dependent nations in its region. While inflation expectation have been largely held under control, analysts will be watching this week's fresh inflation print.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having suffered as a proxy to the spreading unrest in the Middle East, USD/TRY once again tipped above the TRY1.60 mark early Monday, before falling back to TRY1.5985 recently, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In contrast, the Polish zloty inched higher against the euro, up 0.55%, to PLN3.9540, according to FactSet. Despite growing price pressures, Poland's monetary policy authorities are expected to keep interest rates unchanged in a tight vote on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian real edged lower ahead of its policymakers' rate decision on Wednesday. The real was recently at BRL1.6630 against the dollar, from its previous close of BRL1.6620, according to FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Colombian and Chilean pesos slipped, with the latter nation's industrial production data rising slightly less than expected and unemployment increasing a bit more than anticipated. Chile's peso ended at CLP475.80, compared with CLP474.50 on Friday, while trading in a range of CLP474.00 to CLP476.70.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peso fell, with authorities continuing to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through daily U.S. dollar purchases, even after its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly hiked rates last week. On the Embig, Colombia's spread narrowed ten basis points, to 156 basis points over Treasurys, and gained 0.74%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South African rand was once again a top performer, strengthening more than 0.8% against the U.S. dollar on Monday, to ZAR6.9504, according to FactSet. Officials reported on Monday that exports fell 17% in January, while imports climbed 14%, pulling South Africa's trade balance back into deficit territory. South African credit was 22 basis points wider, to 178 basis points over Treasurys. It gained 0.1% on Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not far behind, the Hungarian forint advanced 0.75% against the euro. On Tuesday, Hungary's government is slated to announce its much anticipated fiscal-tightening plans, that includes reducing its debt to escape downgrades to its credit rating that would put the country's bonds in junk territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the week ahead, Brazil is scheduled to release a slew of data, including fourth-quarter GDP, IPCA inflation, and details to its BRL50 billion fiscal spending cut package. Interest rate decisions are also due out of Brazil, Mexico, Poland, and Indonesia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Cynthia Lin, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Anthony Esposito and Margit Feher contributed to this article [ 02-28-11 1657ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110228e72s0015v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
