\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nomura Sees  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Ending\b0\par\par\uc2 1,135 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2011\par 18:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:03 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program that buys $20M each day expires on March 15, and strategists at Normua expect authorities not to renew it. Nomura forecasts a stronger peso down the line, with USD/COP reaching 1760 by year-end. Recent weakness in the Colombian peso can be attributed to the continued US dollar purchases being made by the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , coupled with the fact that regulations prevent local \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermediaries from having direct short USD/COP positions, they said. The dollar was recently at COP1887.7, up from COP1841.2 on Jan. 21. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Visit the Market Talk blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 1659 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke offers a primer on debt to GDP ratios. He notes there's "no magic number" for this measure, but said nations that are between 60% to 70% are "pretty comfortable," and going above 100% is "certainly very concerning." He said he hopes the US can stabilize it's own ration not much higher than current levels. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1636 GMT [Dow Jones] For anyone who wants QE2 to end now, consider the employment landscape that lies before the Fed. Bernanke explains 2.5% GDP is only strong enough to absorb new entrants to the labor force. If that rate were to persist unemployment declines would be "very, very slow." A 3.2% unemployment would lower unemployment to 5% to 6% in 10 years, while an "ambitious" 4.5% growth rate would take three to four years. That's a grim environment, and why so many in the Fed want aggressive action to boost growth. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1633 GMT [Dow Jones] When Bernanke mentioned "price stability" in his comments, that most likely reflected his audience: Congress, which wasn't entirely on board with the Fed's quantitative-easing program, Nomura analysts wrote in a research note. The market didn't take his words to mean that the Fed would tighten monetary policy anytime soon. Bernanke's speech also implied that "the Fed had a positive reaction to the January Employment Report," which showed the unemployment rate dropping to 9% in January when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast the rate wouldn't go that low until the fourth quarter, Nomura said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (chana.schoenberger@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1612 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke just went there with the dollar. He notes the dollar's share of reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been pretty stable, and he says with some of the problems in Europe and elsewhere means "the dollar has been looking a bit more attractive relative to some other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the world." (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1602 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke appears to suggest QE2 will meet its $600 billion target and go no further. Bernanke's said QE2 can likely be brought to an end if the recovery is moving forward and inflation remains low and stable, and said an unwind would likely begin if growth surged and inflation jumped. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1553 GMT (Dow Jones) Hungarian government bonds recover from a 10 bps yield rise in the morning and close hardly changed versus Tuesday's levels as the EUR/USD reverses course and starts to rise. The market will eye Thursday's bond offer to see investor demand ahead of the budget revamp. "The outcome of the offer will set the tone for bonds in the coming days," a trader says. 3-year benchmark yield is 6.85%, 5-year 7.10%, 10-year is at 7.40%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that after the recent rally, the forthcoming revamp of the monetary policy council plus fiscal uncertainties may limit the extent of further gains. EUR/HUF is at 270.70 versus 270.90 in the morning. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1552 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira and bonds are weaker in line with mixed global stock markets due to concerns over inflation and further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy tightening ahead of next week's monetary policy meeting. USD/TRY trades at 1.5867, from 1.5760 Tuesday. Meanwhile the benchmark November 7, 2012 bond ends trade at 8.62% from 8.46% Tuesday, but it moves to 8.46% in Thursday value-dated transactions. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.5655 and 1.6150, and the benchmark bond between 8.42% and 8.65%, in the short term.(yeliz.candemir@iha.com.tr)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1550 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira remains under pressure with USD/TRY recently up 0.6% to 1.5848. "We continue to believe that loss of policy credibility is driving recent TRY underperformance," notes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Win Thin. The losses for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Dec. 16 rate cut and continued with the Jan. 20 cut, he notes, pointing to worries that the bank is moving further behind the inflation curve. "Countries that are trying to avoid outright rate hikes in the face of rising inflation are getting punished for attempting to target too many things," he notes. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. 1-212-416-3100 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1548 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures reached the day's highest prices just after release of Bernanke's prepared remarks on Capitol Hill, then pulled back as Fed chief answers lawmakers' questions. Quarterly 2012 contracts recently up as much as 2 bps, having been as much as 6.5 bps higher at the session high. Nearest 1-yr calendar spread a little tighter as market absorbs Bernanke's comments, meaning market sees short-term rates rising at a slightly slower pace. Mar 2012 contract recently priced in a rate 80.5 bps higher than rate priced into Mar 2011 contract. Same spread at 82.5 bps Tues -- much wider than 73 bps on Mon and 50 bps as recently as settlement on Jan. 28. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1546 GMT [Dow Jones] Tuesday's market action--guided by inflation fears--included increased wagers among fed-funds futures traders for a tightening as soon as the FOMC's Nov. 1-2 meeting. Bernanke's testimony has caused market to reduce those odds. Nov contract recently priced in 44% chance for committee to boost rate to 0.5% in Nov, down from 54% chance at Tues' settlement. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1542 GMT [Dow Jones]Bernanke says it's very unlikely any economic environment could arise that the Fed would lose money on its balance sheet, and he expects the Fed to remain profitable. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. 1-212-416-3100 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-09-11 1203ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110209e729000c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
