\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Colombia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Markey    \par \uc2 1,321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2010\par 19:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Dec 1 (Reuters) - President Juan Manuel Santos' ambitious reform agenda, an uptick in the guerrilla and drugs war, measures to tame the soaring peso and still delicate relations with neighboring Venezuela and Ecuador are key issues to watch in Colombia in the next year.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS' REFORM PROGRAM\par \par \uc2 Since coming to office in August, Santos has pushed an ambitious set of reforms ranging from the management of oil royalties to overhauling the health system and a tax system adjustment. The success of the reforms are seen as essential for Colombia to tackle its fiscal deficit and regain the investment grade it lost in a 1990s fiscal crisis. Key to watch will be the royalties reform, tax overhaul and a fiscal regulation proposal that seeks to lower debt and better savings. Santos has a strong majority in Congress, but several proposals already must have to wait to a second legislative session start in March.\par \par \uc2 The royalties reform has faced resistance from lawmakers allied with government but who represent oil-producing regions. Currently Santos says royalties benefit only a few commodities-producing states and says the reform will centralize how cash is distributed.\par \par \uc2 Colombia is a crude oil producer and the world's No. 5 coal exporter thanks to a fall in guerrilla violence and an increase in foreign investment. Santos is also pushing a fiscal regulation to use surplus oil and mine revenues to cut debt. The reform envisages an overseas saving fund for commodities revenues to help shield the economy during hard times.\par \par \uc2 Local officials in producing regions are already talking about protests if their royalty share is reduced by any reforms. Two lawmakers are also proposing an increase in royalties paid by international companies.\par \par \uc2 That bill has no government backing, but with commodity prices rising it may signal the risk of latent demand for more state share of oil and mining wealth. Some communities are also now protesting by against oil companies to demand payment for infrastructure repairs, and protests may rise as investment increases in rural areas. One protest halted drilling at a Canadian oil company recently.\par \par \uc2 While Santos is adamant he will not raise taxes, some analysts believe some form of tax reform will be inevitable later in his presidency, especially if he is serious about the deficit. A current proposal would lift a tax deduction on some fixed-income investment, bringing an extra $2.6 billion to state offers from 2011.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Protests against oil or mining companies.\par \par \uc2 -- Details on how royalty reform will share out cash.\par \par \uc2 -- Splits in Santos' majority over managing the oil boom.\par \par \uc2 -- How the government handles tax adjustments.\par \par \uc2 MANAGING THE PESO\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen around 8 percent so far this year, hurting exporters who receive revenue in dollars and threatening to chip away at the moderate growth Colombia has seen since the global crisis.\par \par \uc2 The government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have already taken several measures such as holding off bringing back dollars from overseas this year and saying they would use external financing to only pay for overseas obligations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has left the door open for more announcements to try and tame the peso but the finance minister has dismissed the need for now of forceful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 In the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slapped capital controls on short-term portfolio investment to ease the peso's rise and exporters of coffee, oil, coal, flowers and nickel are demanding more measures.\par \par \uc2 But the government is wary of such market-unfriendly moves. Short term measures may not be enough to stem the peso as Colombia receives a boom in oil and mining dollars. But the overseas saving fund planned for oil and mining revenue would in the long term ease appreciation.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcements to curb the peso.\par \par \uc2 -- How Latin America manage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 ARMED GROUPS, DRUG TRAFFICKERS\par \par \uc2 Santos has promised to keep up Uribe's tough stance against left-wing rebels, paramilitary gangs and cocaine traffickers mixed up in Colombia's long conflict. At his swearing-in ceremony, he said the door to dialogue with the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia, or FARC, was "not locked with a key."\par \par \uc2 But his government scored a huge political and military victory in September with a bombing raid that killed top FARC commander Mono Jojoy. That strike will likely spark further desertions from FARC ranks and prompt the government to step up its offensive rather than seek out any dialogue with the FARC. The next target will be Alfonso Cano, the FARC's top commander.\par \par \uc2 The FARC says it wants talks, but Santos, a former defense minister, says rebels must end hostilities, halt criminal activity and release their hostages before any negotiations. Under intense military pressure, the FARC has settled into hit-and-run attacks. It has still shown its military capacity with a recent surge in those attacks.\par \par \uc2 Santos will continue with Uribe's security policies and manage the close alliance with Washington as the White House trims military aid. The FARC could seek to gain credibility by releasing some hostages while trying to show its force by pulling off high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government will try to expand a program to consolidate its military successes by bringing in social and economic development programs quicker to areas hit hardest by conflict and drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Colombia remains the world's No. 1 cocaine producer and multiple illegal armed groups are all engaged in the drug trade, making the government's task more complex. Armed groups have fragmented and rebels have formed alliances with drug-trafficking gangs and former paramilitaries.\par \par \uc2 Despite the demobilization of paramilitary groups who once fought the FARC, new crime groups have emerged and rights groups say they are an increasing threat to vulnerable, rural towns and some urban areas where small-time drug-trafficking is common. A car bomb in Bogota soon after Santos came to office was a reminder of risks. Markets mainly dismissed the bombing, but a sustained increase in urban attacks would unnerve investors.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Blows to FARC leadership, large-scale desertions.\par \par \uc2 -- Urban attacks by FARC to show presence.\par \par \uc2 -- Evidence of alliances between armed groups.\par \par \uc2 ANDEAN NEIGHBORS\par \par \uc2 Tensions between Colombia and its left-wing Andean neighbors, Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez and Ecuador's President Rafael Correa, have eased considerably since Santos took office after more than two years of diplomatic disputes.\par \par \uc2 The new Colombian leader has taken a very pragmatic approach. Chavez and Santos met just days after Santos was sworn in and the two promised to restore diplomatic ties, resume trade and seek payment of around $800 million that Venezuela owes to Colombian exporters. Since then, several initiatives such as cooperation against drug-traffickers and guerrilla presence in Venezuela have underscored improving relations.\par \par \uc2 Both countries could use a resumption of their $7 billion a year in bilateral trade. But Santos and Chavez have bickered in the past and underlying differences between U.S. ally Colombia and Washington adversary Venezuela remain unresolved. That may stir up tensions again. Some analysts question whether Venezuela has the cash to pay debt owed to Colombian companies, and Colombian exporters are wary of returning wholesale to the Venezuelan market after prolonged trade restrictions.\par \par \uc2 With Ecuador's Correa too Santos has made headway with the two governments announcing their intention to fully restore ties frayed two years ago over a Colombian bombing raid on a FARC rebel camp across Ecuador's border.\par \par \uc2 Colombia has helped supply Ecuador with much-needed electricity in the past and continues to do so. But FARC rebels crossing into Ecuador will likely remain a point of tension.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- How Santos manages any flare-ups in relations again.\par \par \uc2 -- Stalled bilateral trade and Colombia debt payments. (Editing by Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101201e6c1001c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
