\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Colombia's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  keeps key rate at 3 pct\b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2010\par 19:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Decision in line with unanimous poll forecast\par \par \uc2 * Inflation target set at 2 to 4 percent for 2011\par \par \uc2 * Cenbank sees inflation ending 2011 at lower target limit\par \par \uc2 * No mention of any new peso measures at meeting (Adds details, comment, analyst)\par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday maintained its benchmark interest rate at 3 percent, in line with market expectations, and set its 2011 inflation target at between 2 percent and 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 All 45 analysts consulted by Reuters this week had forecast the bank would keep the same rate as the authority has done since April this year due to low inflation expectations and a recovery in economic growth after the 2009 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's monetary body said on Friday the 2011 inflation target would be the same as 2010 at between 2 percent and 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The available inflation projections suggest that upon ending 2011, inflation will be situated at a level near the lower limit of the target range," it said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Last year's inflation rate was 2.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 Around Latin America this month, Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  upped its benchmark rate by 25 basis points to 3 percent while Peru's authority held its rate steady at 3 percent as inflation showed benign increases. [ID:nLIP002084] [ID:nN16125546] <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 For more Latin American interest rates [ID:nN14153782]\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on Colombia's battle with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/kuv25q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/kuv25q}}}\par \par \uc2 For a graphic of Latin America inflation trends:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/peb64q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/peb64q}}}\uc2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the bank to keep its rate at 3 percent for at least the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to derive significant comfort from a macro environment characterized by moderate growth taking place against a backdrop of still negative output gap and benevolent current and expected inflation dynamics," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analyst Alberto Ramos said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Furthermore, domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation has turned into a valuable inflation moderating force by anchoring tradable goods inflation in negative territory."\par \par \uc2 NO MENTION OF NEW PESO MEASURES\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets are bracing for a wave of investment from yield-seeking investors after the U.S. Federal Reserve said it would spend $600 billion buying bonds in a second wave of quantitative easing designed to keep down U.S. rates.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not mention any new measures against the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and none of the analysts polled by Reuters had expected any new steps to help curb the peso.\par \par \uc2 "There are some analysts that would like us to make large \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... and even give examples like the case of Brazil," bank chief Jose Dario Uribe said at a news conference. "In our case, we've seen through history that the most effective \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ones we're doing now."\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry told Reuters earlier this month that Colombia would try to avoid imposing Brazil-style capital controls to curtail the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but "nothing is ruled out." [ID:nN12127423]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying at least $20 million a day in a program, scheduled to last until mid-March, meant curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has surged 8.8 percent this year; the government has also announced mainly structural measures.\par \par \uc2 Since early October, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened around 4.5 percent on those measures and fears of more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Uribe said earlier this month the bank had not ruled out the possibility of "big" \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN05292894]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it had not exhausted all \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures and would only use external financing next year to meet outside obligations. [ID:nN16108112] (Writing by Jack Kimball; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RATES/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101119e6bj0019o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
