\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  eyes "big"  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2010\par 20:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Central banker's first reference to "big \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 "\par \par \uc2 * Countries around the world move to control \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Adds context, analyst's quote)\par \par \uc2 By Hugh Bronstein\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Nov 5 (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not rule out the possibility of "big" \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to control the strength of the local peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jose Dario Uribe said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not worked in the past does not mean that they might not work in the future. In fact, we do not discard the possibility of big \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Uribe said in televised remarks.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying at least $20 million per day in a program, scheduled to last until mid-March, meant to take some of the juice out of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <COP=RR>. Since the year began, the peso has climbed 11 percent against the U.S. dollar to 1,821 per greenback.\par \par \uc2 Friday's comment was the first in which Uribe, usually known for using measured language, spoke of "big \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." But local market players did not read the comment as indicating an immediate increase in dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 "I am betting that the bank will continue relying on verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as this one," said Daniel Velandia, chief economist at Bogota brokerage Correval.\par \par \uc2 "He is making the point that they are serious about controlling the peso and reaffirming that while $20 million per day is the minimum, that minimum could be raised, if necessary," Velandia said.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers worldwide are moving to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening as investors -- turning their backs on low interest rates in developed economies -- pour money into higher-yielding emerging markets. The measures have been wide ranging.\par \par \uc2 The waters of the debate have been muddied by the Federal Reserve's decision, announced this week, to buy $600 billion in long-term bonds with new money in an effort to revive the flagging U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Brazil, warning of a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," criticized the U.S. plan and has raised taxes on foreigners buying local bonds for the second time since the start of October.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION SEEN UNDER 3 PERCENT\par \par \uc2 Policymakers in Bogota have avoided imposing capital controls as they campaign for Colombia to win back the investment-grade credit rating that the country lost in an economic crisis 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 But political pressure to ease the peso's rise remains strong. And Colombian businesses that sell their products overseas are clamoring for policymakers to do more.\par \par \uc2 Exporters who get paid in dollars while having to pay their costs in pesos say they are being forced to lay off workers as profits shrink along with the once-strong dollar.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's inflation rate has a "high probability" of being under 3 percent this year, Uribe also said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The bank's target range for inflation this year is between 2 percent and 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We see a high probability that inflation will be under 3 percent this year," Uribe said. "Why will we have inflation under 3 percent? For the appreciation of the peso, because the economy has excess capacity and due to the close of trade with Venezuela."\par \par \uc2 Venezuela has shut off trade with neighboring Colombia as part of a diplomatic spat over Bogota's close military alliance with Washington. The border's closing has slowed Colombia's recovery from the 2008-09 world crisis.\par \par \uc2 Colombia expects its economy to expand 4.5 percent this year and in 2011. Uribe said Colombia's inflation rate will be within the official target range.\par \par \uc2 "Our technical team is projecting an inflation rate not very far from 2 percent in the year ahead," Uribe said.\par \par \uc2 The bank's official inflation projection for 2011 is between 1.8 percent and 3 percent. (Additional reporting by Nelson Bocanegra and Javier Mozzo; Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-ECONOMY (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101105e6b5001wr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
