\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Keeps Rate at 3%; Extends  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2010\par 00:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with analysts comments)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Darcy Crowe \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its benchmark interest rate at 3% for a sixth straight month on Friday while also unveiling an extension of its dollar purchases in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to rein in the appreciation of the peso.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will buy a minimum of $20 million daily until at least May 15, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Jose Dario Uribe said.\par \par \uc2 Potential measures by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been widely expected by the market, with the peso retreating against the dollar this week after President Juan Manuel Santos said Saturday that the government would soon announce new steps to rein in the peso.\par \par \uc2 "The measures announced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came short of the market expectation," Alberto Ramos, an economist with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's buying program was previously slated to last until January 15.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started buying $20 million daily on Sept. 15 to rein in the peso's surge, a decision which Uribe said had been widely successful. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far purchased $580 million in the second half of the year, Uribe said\par \par \uc2 Despite the September decision, a group of leading business groups asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct. 11 to step up its efforts against the peso's surge, which is threatening exporters.\par \par \uc2 The peso's climb, which nears 12% this year, "puts at risk the positive economic outlook for the Colombian economy," the business groups said in an open letter. The business groups also asked the bank to consider lowering its benchmark rate in a bid to weaken the peso.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained against the dollar this year in part fueled by foreign investments in the country's booming oil and mining industries. Foreign direct investment is expected to reach $10 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 Some economists warned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would unveil capital-control mechanisms to limit the number of dollars entering the Colombian economy and stem the peso's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Other observers also said authorities could limit the short-term borrowing abroad by local firms to discourage them from bringing dollars into Colombia.\par \par \uc2 Uribe reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had weighed the costs and benefits of capital controls and had decided they weren't currently justified.\par \par \uc2 Before the announcement on Friday the peso closed at COP1,840.00 to the dollar, weakening from 1,836.99.\par \par \uc2 Alianza Valores, a local brokerage, said that the market's initial reaction will strengthen the peso to as much as COP1,815.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also widely expected to keep its benchmark rate unchanged at 3%. "Inflation has been in line with our expectations," Uribe said, adding that faster economic growth isn't pressuring prices. The Colombian economy will likely grow 4.5% in 2011, Uribe said.\par \par \uc2 Price increases this year have stayed within the lower range of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% to 4% target. Consumer prices in September declined 0.14% as a result of drops in food prices. Inflation over the 12 months through September was 2.28%.\par \par \uc2 After emerging from a slowdown last year, the Colombian economy picked up speed and grew 4.5% in the second quarter. Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry has said that the gross domestic product could expand 5% this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Darcy Crowe, Dow Jones Newswires; (57) 1 703 8953; darcy.crowe@dowjones.com [ 10-29-10 1817ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101029e6at0010x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
