\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Colombia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Markey   \par \uc2 1,311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Oct 1 (Reuters) - President Juan Manuel Santos' ambitious reform agenda, an uptick in the country's guerrilla and drugs war, a soaring peso and still delicate relations with neighboring Venezuela and Ecuador are key issues to watch in Colombia in the next year.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS REFORM PROGRAM\par \par \uc2 Since taking office last month, Santos has proposed an ambitious set of reforms ranging from the management of oil and mining royalties, and agrarian land reform, to overhauling the health and justice systems.\par \par \uc2 Key for Colombia's attempts to tackle its stubborn fiscal deficit -- seen at 4.4 percent of GDP this year -- will be royalties reform [ID:nN31245717] and a fiscal regulation proposal [ID:nN07144747], which are both before Congress.\par \par \uc2 Santos has a strong majority in Congress, but the royalties reform is already facing resistance from lawmakers allied with government but who represent oil-producing regions.\par \par \uc2 Currently the Santos administration says royalties benefit only a few commodities-producing states under a system rife with mismanagement. The royalty reform will centralize how cash is distributed so that all regions benefit.\par \par \uc2 It also seeks to stamp out inefficiency and corruption as crude production grows to an estimated 1.5 million barrels per day within a few years.\par \par \uc2 Local officials in producing regions are already talking about protests if their royalty share is reduced. Investors are keen to see Colombia better manage its deficit and debt as steps to recovering investment grade with rating agencies after it lost the status during the 1990s. [ID:nN16265600]\par \par \uc2 Colombia is a significant oil producer and the world's No. 5 coal producer thanks to a sharp fall in guerrilla war violence under Santos's predecessor, Alvaro Uribe. The fiscal regulation, proposed by Uribe's government and adopted by Santos, looks to use surplus oil and mining revenues to cut debt and tackle the deficit. Later, the reform envisages an overseas saving fund for commodities revenues to help shield the economy during hard times.\par \par \uc2 Constitutional reforms also take time with eight debates required in the two-chamber Congress. While Santos is adamant he will not raise taxes, some analysts believe some form of tax reform will be inevitable later in his presidency, especially if he is serious about the deficit. [ID:nN30181910]\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO WATCH:\par \par \uc2 -- Protests in oil-producing states as reform advances.\par \par \uc2 -- Splits in Santos' majority over managing the oil boom.\par \par \uc2 -- Any shift on how to manage tax reforms.\par \par \uc2 ARMED GROUPS, DRUG TRAFFICKERS\par \par \uc2 Santos has promised to keep up Uribe's tough stance against left-wing rebels, paramilitary gangs and cocaine traffickers mixed up in Colombia's long conflict. At his swearing-in ceremony, he said the door to dialogue with the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia, or FARC, was "not locked with a key." [ID:nWEN8602].\par \par \uc2 But his government scored a huge political and military victory in September with a bombing raid that killed top FARC commander Mono Jojoy [ID:nN23139058]. That strike will likely spark further desertions from FARC ranks and prompt the government to step up its offensive rather than seek out any dialogue with the FARC. [ID:nN24193042]. The next target will be Alfonso Cano, the FARC's top commander.\par \par \uc2 The FARC says it still wants talks, but Santos, a former defense minister, says rebels must end hostilities, halt criminal activity and release their hostages before any negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Under intense military pressure, the FARC has settled into hit-and-run attacks. It has, however, still shown its military capacity with a recent surge in those attacks. [ID:nN19273057]\par \par \uc2 Santos will continue with Uribe's security policies and manage the close alliance with Washington as the White House trims military aid. The FARC could seek to gain credibility by releasing some hostages while trying to show its force by pulling off high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The government will try to expand a program to consolidate its military successes by bringing in social and economic development programs quicker to areas hit hardest by conflict and drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 But Colombia remains the world's No. 1 cocaine producer and multiple illegal armed groups are all engaged in the drug trade, making the government's task more complex. Armed groups have fragmented and rebels have formed alliances with drug-trafficking gangs and former paramilitaries.\par \par \uc2 Defense officials say that could give the FARC a short-term life-line and lead to a brief spike of violence as military pressure increases. Despite the demobilization of paramilitary groups who once fought the FARC, new crime groups have emerged and rights groups say they are an increasing threat to vulnerable, rural towns and villages.\par \par \uc2 A car bomb in Bogota soon after Santos came to office was a reminder of security risks, though authorities have yet to blame any specific group [ID:nN12273335]. Markets mainly dismissed the bombing, but a sustained increase in urban attacks would unnerve investors.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO WATCH:\par \par \uc2 -- Further blows to the FARC's leadership, large-scale desertions.\par \par \uc2 -- Urban attacks by FARC to show presence.\par \par \uc2 -- Evidence of alliances between armed groups\par \par \uc2 MANAGING THE PESO\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen around 12 percent so far this year, hurting exporters who receive revenue in dollars and threatening to undermine the moderate growth Colombia has seen since the global crisis. That has worried the government, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently began a program to purchase dollars to curb appreciation. [ID:nN15145483]. It left the door open for more measures and at a recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, the board said more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was possible alongside other mechanisms.\par \par \uc2 In the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slapped capital controls on short-term portfolio investment to ease the peso's rise [ID:nN24243857] and exporters of coffee, oil, coal, flowers and nickel are already demanding more measures. [ID:nN16117081].\par \par \uc2 Short term measures may not be enough to stem the peso as Colombia receives a boom in oil and mining dollars. But the overseas saving fund planned for oil and mining revenue would in the long term ease appreciation.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO WATCH:\par \par \uc2 -- New measures from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or government.\par \par \uc2 -- How other countries in Latin America manage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 ANDEAN NEIGHBORS\par \par \uc2 Tensions between Colombia and its left-wing Andean neighbors, Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez and Ecuador's President Rafael Correa, have eased since Santos took office after more than two years of diplomatic disputes.\par \par \uc2 Chavez and Santos met just days after Santos was sworn in and the two promised to restore diplomatic ties, resume trade and seek payment of around $800 million that Venezuela owes to Colombian exporters. [ID:nN10132853]\par \par \uc2 Both countries need a resumption of their $7 billion a year in bilateral trade [ID:nN10179861]. But Santos and Chavez have bickered in the past and underlying differences between U.S. ally Colombia and Washington adversary Venezuela remain unresolved. That will likely stir up tensions again [ID:nN11231503].\par \par \uc2 Colombia has accused Venezuela of allowing FARC guerrillas safe haven on its soil. Some analysts question whether Venezuela has the cash to pay debt owed to Colombian companies, and Colombian exporters are wary of returning wholesale to the Venezuelan market after prolonged restrictions on commerce and concerns over future spats. [ID:nN11224605]\par \par \uc2 Relations with Ecuador have improved since a 2008 crisis sparked by a Colombian army raid across its border to attack a FARC rebel camp. Under Santos, the two governments have held talks, especially since Colombia has helped supply Ecuador with much-needed electricity.\par \par \uc2 A local Ecuadorean judge has also revoked an arrest warrant against Santos for ordering the 2008 raid, when he was Colombia's defense minister, opening the way for a presidential meeting.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO WATCH:\par \par \uc2 -- How Chavez, Santos manage their fledgling detente.\par \par \uc2 -- Stalled bilateral trade and debt payments.\par \par \uc2 -- A meeting between Santos and Correa. (Editing by Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101001e6a10017o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
