\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Weaker As  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 17:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:06 (Dow Jones) Colombian peso weakens as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it'll buy $20M a day on the spot market to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and build up international reserves. Peso eases to COP1,805.45 recently from COP1,790.35. However, local brokerage Interbolsa says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move won't lead to sustained correction as flow of foreign direct investment in Colombia is still high. Yield on benchmark 2020 bonds falls to 7.225% from 7.300% Tuesday. Benchmark IGBC stock index recently down a fraction to 14182. (inti.landauro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:02 (Dow Jones) With today marking the anniversary of Lehman's bankruptcy filing, the trajectory of the stock market during the past two years has been one wild ride. Bespoke Investment Group notes consumer discretionary and technology are the only two sectors trading above pre-Lehman levels. And while financials have rallied 140% off March 2009 bottom, they still need to gain another 43% "before they can put the pain of the financial crisis behind them," firm says. Overall, S&P 500 remains 12% lower than it was two years ago. S&P 500 recently up less than a point. (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bespokeinvest.com/thinkbig/2010/9/14/the-miracle-of-time-lehman-two-years-later.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.bespokeinvest.com/thinkbig/2010/9/14/the-miracle-of-time-lehman-two-years-later.html}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 10:59 (Dow Jones) Susquehanna says \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 's (YHOO) sale of its 39% stake in Alibaba "could be imminent." Firm says conflict of interest between YHOO and the Chinese e-commerce firm is driving the sale because the two will be in direct competition if YHOO follows through with plans to sell ads to Chinese companies. Stock could benefit from the sale, firm says. "In our view, the monetization of \uc2 Alibaba Group\uc2  assets may enable new investment in YHOO shares, potentially attracting value investors unwilling to invest while YHOO was a conglomerate (with little to no control over investments representing as much as 43% of its net income)." YHOO not immediately available for comment. Shares up 1.5% to $13.84. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:53 (Dow Jones) How little times change -- for some. \uc2 US Bancorp\uc2  (USB) CEO Richard David updated investors today about his bank's goals during the first investor day in three years. The goals are "only a slight variation" from what was said back then, Davis said. Despite crisis delevering and regulatory scrutiny of the banking industry, USB expects to generate up to 19% return of equity -- one percentage point less than three years ago; and 8% to 10% EPS growth, compared to a 10% goal previously. Davis was less upbeat about the economy. "It's a very, very slow recovery. It's going to take a couple of years," he said. USB down 2.5% at $22.69. (matthias.rieker@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:45 (Dow Jones) Chile's peso slightly weaker against the US dollar as markets were stirred up after Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than six years. "Participants this morning are a little more cautious when they see a country like Japan coming out to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. Peso recently weaker at CLP494.00 vs CLP493.50 at yesterday's close. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:40 (Dow Jones) Americans are $6.6 trillion short of the pension and retirement savings they ought to have today in order to maintain their standard of living when they retire, according to data calculated by the Center for Retirement Research at Boston College. That so-called "retirement income deficit" is five times the projected federal budget deficit for 2010, according to Retirement USA, a group of organizations advocating for strengthening social security and improving private retirement sources. (lynn.cowan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:34 (Dow Jones) FDA advisory committee meets today to consider whether to recommend withdrawal of \uc2 Abbott Labs\uc2 ' (ABT) Meridia diet drug from market over safety concerns. Although it's an important medical issue, it's barely registering on the Street's radar due to drug's modest sales ($300M worldwide in '09). At \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  conference Monday, ABT's CFO didn't mention issue and he wasn't asked about it by analysts or investors. They were more focused on cost control, M&A, and ABT's biggest drug, Humira. ABT up a fraction at $51.54. (peter.loftus@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:28 (Dow Jones) \uc2 UBS\uc2  takes stake in Mantara Inc., a New Jersey tech company catering to high-frequency trading firms, touting its systems for doing business across multiple asset classes. Bank will offer Mantara's products and services to customers looking to ramp up high-speed trading capabilities -- as well as its systems to help control risk involved when trading moves in the milliseconds. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 ("MARKET TALK: Stocks Ply 'Critical' Juncture" published at 10:19 a.m. EDT misidentified Art Cashin's firm. A corrected version follows.)\par \par \uc2 (Dow Jones) Yesterday's trading featured some puzzling relationships, Art Cashin at \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes in his morning note. Treasurys rallied, gold rose sharply -- typically signs of move to safety, but the dollar weakened. Meanwhile, stocks meandered between small gains and losses throughout the day. "By early afternoon, traders were looking at the tape like it was a Picasso painting," he writes. "For equities, the net result was yet another low volume stall at the top of the trading range." Now, Cashin says the market may be at a "very critical inflection point," especially as S&P 500 stalled right below top of its three-month trading range for a second-straight day. S&P 500 flat at 1121; Cashin sees support at 1111-1113 range. (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:19 (Dow Jones) Yesterday's trading featured some puzzling relationships, Art Cashin at USB notes in his morning note. Treasurys rallied, gold rose sharply -- typically signs of move to safety, but the dollar weakened. Meanwhile, stocks meandered between small gains and losses throughout the day. "By early afternoon, traders were looking at the tape like it was a Picasso painting," he writes. "For equities, the net result was yet another low volume stall at the top of the trading range." Now, Cashin says the market may be at a "very critical inflection point," especially as S&P 500 stalled right below top of its three-month trading range for a second-straight day. S&P 500 flat at 1121; Cashin sees support at 1111-1113 range. (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:07 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Genzyme\uc2  (GENZ) reiterates that \uc2 Sanofi-Aventis\uc2 ' (SNY) $69/share takeover offer is too low during a presentation to a BofA Merrill conference in London. SNY's valuation of GENZ underestimates some key pieces of the business as well as its competitive advantage in treating rare diseases, said \uc2 John Butler\uc2 , head of GENZ's personalized genetic health business. Among sources of future value, he cited Campath, which GENZ is testing in MS, and revival of orphan disease treatments that've been hurt by manufacturing and regulatory setbacks. GENZ up a fraction at $70.41. (thomas.gryta@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:04 (Dow Jones) Saks (SKS) CEO Steve Sadove does not rule out a buyout, saying "We have always been very focused on shareholder value." Speaking at a \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  retail conference, Sadove added that in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "I don't believe if we were private we would do anything different than we do today." The comments came after reports that two private equity firms are considering teaming up to buy SKS, which struggled mightily during the recession, but has turned \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  around. Shares up 1.5% at $8.36. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-15-10 1106ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100915e69f000bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
