\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ Colombian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Expands Arsenal To Fight Peso Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Darcy Crowe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 00:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be expanding its arsenal to combat the appreciation of the peso, potentially making it more difficult for investors to assess the scope of the bank's presence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry have made it known to the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an array of tools at its disposal to rein in the peso's 13% surge against the dollar so far this year. It remains to be seen, however, whether these additional weapons are sufficient to stop the peso's advance.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traditionally purchased dollars in the spot market and then tried to neutralize the inflationary effect of putting more pesos in circulation by selling government-issued bonds, known as TES, in the secondary market to mop up liquidity. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's TES portfolio served as an indicator of how long the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue buying dollars and selling TES to limit inflation, Echeverry said.\par \par \uc2 The TES held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "indicated how much fuel it had" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Echeverry said. "Some investors would see this and say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only buy dollars for three months, so they would wait, (and) after that they would come back to the market."\par \par \uc2 "We've made that calculation very difficult," he added.\par \par \uc2 Among the possible new tools the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has available is the issuance of its own bonds, which along with the TES could serve to damp any excess liquidity created by the sale of pesos for dollar purchases in the spot market. Another possibility is the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options or discretionary purchases in the spot market.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry, however, has declined to specify which specific tools the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out daily dollar purchases in the spot market to the tune of $1.6 billion for the first six months of 2009. That strategy will likely not be enough to stop the peso, which continues to gain against the dollar on massive inflows of direct investment into \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be very smart to have any real effect on the market," said Julian Cardenas, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with local brokerage firm Corredores Asociados. "It appears that they are examining a combination of mechanisms" to curb the peso, he added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappointed several economists and analysts who had expected it would announce the start of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its policy meeting last month. The bank's board of directors, which includes Echeverry, is slated to meet again on Sept. 23.\par \par \uc2 A potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even with additional tools, is unlikely to be sufficient to reverse the peso's gains, said Cardenas. A surge in foreign direct investment, which is expected to reach $10 billion this year, is behind the peso's strength. On Monday, the peso traded at COP1,791.3 a dollar, its strongest since October 2009.\par \par \uc2 The peso's strength has raised the specter of capital controls, which in the past has failed to curb the peso's appreciation. The risk of capital controls is climbing because of the peso's strength, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Win Thin, with \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 , wrote in a research note.\par \par \uc2 For others, however, that remains unlikely. "They have other alternatives and using capital controls could be damaging at a time when the country wants foreign investment," Cardenas said.\par \par \uc2 -By Darcy Crowe, Dow Jones Newswires; (57) 1 703 8953; darcy.crowe@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 13, 2010 18:12 ET (22:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100913e69d005s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
