\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico, Colombia pesos hit by US housing data\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2010\par 15:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Surprisingly large drop in U.S. housing data weighs\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso hits seven-week low\par \par \uc2 * Weak markets ease \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns in \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Recasts, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - A surprisingly big drop in U.S. housing sales reported on Tuesday weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico and Colombia, the two Latin American economies that could suffer the most from slowing growth in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Sales of U.S. existing homes in July fell to their slowest pace in 15 years, AN industry group reported. For details, see [ID:nN24249810]\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso slumped to its weakest since July 7.\par \par \uc2 "This was another disappointing number for the markets and it provides more signs that, definitely, in the United States the economy is slowing down," said Gabriel Casillas, an economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The United States is the top trading partner of both Mexico and Colombia, making them the most vulnerable in the region to a decrease in U.S. demand for their exports.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> lost 0.32 percent to 12.9705 per U.S. dollar. The peso briefly traded above the psychological level of 13 per dollar before paring losses.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP=STFX> lost 0.31 percent to bid at 1,819 per dollar. Speculation has been mounting that Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could wade into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem sharp gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, growing fears of a U.S. slowdown could help weaken the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "If the markets, particularly in the United States, are preforming badly, they may be able to delay announcing something," said David Beker, head of Latin American fixed income strategy at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL, CHILE RECOVER\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> firmed 0.11 percent to bid at 1.763 per dollar on the local spot market, recovering from a one-month low hit earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Sao Paulo said exporters had made the most of the morning's jitters to buy the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at cheap levels, which helped cap losses.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors may also have been tempted to buy the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level in order to buy local bonds and take advantage of Brazil's relatively high interest rates, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The real has also been boosted by optimism over inflows, partly due to the upcoming massive share offering by Brazil's state-run oil company, \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "When the dollar was at a strong level, that ended up attracting inflows," said Jose Carlos Amado, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at the Renascenca brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=> firmed a slight 0.06 percent to bid at 504.20 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The head of Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the peso was trading in line with current fundamentals, dampening concerns of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following sharp gains in the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN24252365]\par \par \uc2 Bond spreads indicated that investors had become more wary of the region's higher-risk assets following the U.S. data.\par \par \uc2 Yield spreads between emerging market bonds and U.S. Treasuries, a key gauge of risk aversion, widened 15 basis points to 283 basis points, according to the \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  EMBI+ index <11EMJ>.\par \par \uc2 The spread on Brazilian bonds was at the widest level since late last month, rising 11 basis points to 212. (Additional reporting by Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo and Jean Luis Arce in Mexico City; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100827e68o000zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
