\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  peso up as c.bank eschews  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 15:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso gains 1 pct after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso posts weekly losses due to U.S. concerns\par \par \uc2 * Slowing inflation backs bets of no more hikes in Brazil (Recasts; adds comments, byline; previous dateline SAO PAULO/BOGOTA)\par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA/MEXICO CITY, Aug 20 (Reuters) - Colombia's peso firmed sharply on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from introducing any measures to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong gains as many had expected.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso surged 1 percent to bid at 1,803 per dollar in so-called "next day" trade, compared with its official close <COP=STFX> before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold on Friday. While policy-makers said they were studying possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, they did not announce any concrete actions, as widely expected. [ID:nN20139133]\par \par \uc2 "Independent of what is happening in external markets, we are going to see a rise in the peso at the start of next week," said Santiago Melo, an analyst with brokerage Alianza Valores.\par \par \uc2 Huge inflows into the Andean country's mining and oil sectors and confidence Colombia will soon regain its investment grade credit rating have boosted the peso by around 12 percent since early May.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the Colombia peso is crimping profits from the country's exporters of goods like flowers and manufactured products.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the peso's strength stemmed from factors like improved trade and foreign direct investment, which suggests policy-makers may not implement an aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 However, the bank, known as Banrep, may act if the pace of appreciation increases.\par \par \uc2 "We think that even if Banrep seems willing to tolerate an appreciation trend as long as it seems warranted by macroeconomic conditions, it seems unwilling to allow for this appreciation to be excessively fast or sharp," wrote \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  analysts Roberto Melzi and Jimena Zuniga in a note.\par \par \uc2 Melzi and Zuniga expect Colombia's peso to firm to 1700 per dollar by year end.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL SLIPS ON PETROBRAS, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Fears over slowing U.S. growth, following weak data on jobs and manufacturing on Thursday, weighed on Brazil's real and Mexico's peso.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> lost 0.33 percent to 12.7525 per U.S. dollar. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also held its main lending rate at 4.5 percent as expected, in an effort to stimulate growth. [ID:nN20137407]\par \par \uc2 U.S. slowdown concerns often weigh the most weigh on Mexico, since it sends most of its exports to its northern neighbor. The Mexican peso lost 0.24 percent during the week.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over the timing of the upcoming share offering by Brazil's state-run oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , expected to bring vast amounts of dollars to Brazil, has also hit the real. [ID:nN19264213]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's finance minister, \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2 , said on Friday the government is maintaining a September target date for \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 ' capitalization plan.\par \par \uc2 On the local spot market, the real <BRBY> weakened 0.17 percent to bid at 1.758 per dollar. It still gained close to 0.7 percent for the week. [ID:nN20135046]\par \par \uc2 An unexpected drop in Brazil's consumer prices in the month to mid-August added to signs that growth in Latin America's largest economy is slowing more than previously forecast. [ID:nN20125845]\par \par \uc2 Yields plunged on Brazilian interest rate futures <0#DIJ:> as investors bet the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be less likely to raise the cost of borrowing over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The market shows traders expect no rate hike at the conclusion of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting on September 1, and are betting on no rate increases next year either.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=CL>, which has also been hit by recent threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reversed early losses to firm more than 0.66 percent at a bid of 504.00 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained almost 1.1 percent during the week, boosted by prospects for solid growth and further interest rate hikes as the country rebuilds from a major earthquake. (Additional reporting Samantha Pearson in Sao Paulo; Editing by Richard Chang)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100823e68k000vj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
