\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 , Chile peso dip on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 15:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Chilean peso hits five-month high after rate hike\par \par \uc2 * Colombian peso hurt by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk\par \par \uc2 * Rise in early August U.S. consumer confidence supports\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso firms 0.06 pct, Brazil's real up 0.17 pct\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Colombia's peso weakened sharply on Friday on fears of government market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Chile's peso reversed early gains one day after an interest rate hike.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso sank for a second straight session on concerns local authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cool sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that are hurting local exporters. The peso <COP=STFX> shed 0.51 percent to 1,835.70 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled back about 1.6 percent after hitting a two-year intraday high in the previous session after Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos met with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials to discuss the peso's strong appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "The official rhetorical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally pushed the Colombian peso into defensive mode," wrote Enrique Alvarez, head of research at consultancy IDEAglobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained about 11 percent since early May, boosted by investment flows into the country's oil and mining industries -- also drawing speculative traders.\par \par \uc2 Analysts think Colombia could reinstate daily dollar purchases that expired in June, but that would do little more than slow the pace of gains.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso hit a five-month high Friday, a day after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave up ground as traders locked in gains ahead of the expected rate hike.\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark interest rate to 2 percent, in line with expectations as the economy bounces back from a devastating earthquake. For more, see [ID:nN12145806]\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso's <CLP=CL> opened at its strongest level since early March, but reversed early gains to bid flat at 509.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has firmed about 7 percent since the end of June. Chilean President Sebastian Pinera hinted Thursday the government could cash in less dollar revenues from copper sales to offset the flows into Chilean assets. [ID:nN12124343]\par \par \uc2 But analysts looked for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to firm further on expectations of more interest rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is seen taking its benchmark rate to 3.75 percent by year-end. [ID:nN12129420]\par \par \uc2 BOND DEMAND\par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer sentiment inched up in early August from July, providing support to Mexico's peso and Brazil's real [ID:N13180040]\par \par \uc2 The data came after another report showed retail sales rebounded in July and eased concerns about the slowdown in the United States, Mexico's top trading partner and a major influence on the region.\par \par \uc2 But analysts said the Mexican peso would be far more sensitive to any negative data, given concerns of renewed recession after more signs of a slowdown in U.S. growth.\par \par \uc2 "Private consumption is a very important issue," said Daniela Blancas, a strategist at Scotia Capital in Mexico City. "This is the main worry about economies, above all in the United States."\par \par \uc2 Flows into Mexican peso bonds have also been boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , following a decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve to continue buying Treasury bonds and hold down its benchmark interest rate at its near-zero level for an extended period.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's benchmark 10-year peso bond <MX10YT=RR> bid down 1 basis point to 6.32 percent trading close to a record low hit in the previous session. Mexican bonds have been posting record lows since May.\par \par \uc2 The meager yields on U.S. Treasuries are giving investors more reason to buy higher-yielding emerging market debt.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> hit a session high of 12.6825 per U.S. dollar after the U.S. consumer confidence data, but pulled back to trade at 12.7235 per U.S. dollar, 0.06 percent firmer.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> bid 0.17 percent stronger at 1.769 per dollar. (Additional reporting by Simon Gardner in Santiago and Jean Luis Arce in Mexico City; editing by Jeffrey Benkoe)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100816e68d000yt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
