\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Threat To Buy Dollars Helped Halt Peso Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Bristow   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2010\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the rise of the peso--the mere threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was enough.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought government bonds worth 3 trillion Colombian pesos (US$1.53 billion) towards the end of 2009, the bank said in a statement Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In late October, the bank announced it would spend COP3 trillion on dollars and government bonds to meet seasonal demand for liquidity. The bank spent the entire sum on bonds.\par \par \uc2 "They didn't need to buy a single dollar," said Felipe Munoz, who manages a fund of dollar-bonds with local brokerage Corredores Asociados. "Just the threat was enough to give them all the devaluation they needed."\par \par \uc2 When the bank announced its bond and dollar purchase program, the peso was approaching COP1,800 against the dollar, provoking complaints from exporters who said the strong peso was making it difficult for them to compete. The announcement helped bring to an end a peso rally where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained 41% in about six months.\par \par \uc2 Buying bonds rather than dollars had been a better use of the bank's ammunition because it helped reduce long-term interest rates, said Alberto Bernal, a Miami-based debt strategist with investment bank Bulltick Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Monetarily speaking it's the same, but buying bonds has a more direct effect on the long end of the (yield) curve. So you achieve one goal which is to flatten the yield curve by buying bonds, the effect is more direct," Bernal said.\par \par \uc2 Lowering long-term yields would help the housing market, among other benefits, Bernal added.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Colombia's benchmark peso-denominated government bond maturing in 2020 fell to 8.022% by mid-November, from 8.59% when the bank made its announcement. However, it then began to rise again, and ended the year at 8.467%.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at COP1,962.15 on Tuesday, from COP1,963.50 on January 8.\par \par \uc2 Munoz of Corredores Asociados said the peso could appreciate to COP1,850 against the dollar in the coming weeks, as the Colombian government trades dollars it raised by selling dollar-denominated bonds for pesos.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian treasury placed the equivalent of $2.5 billion in bonds on international markets in 2009, of which $2 billion will be spent in 2010. Since most government spending is in pesos, the treasury sells dollars for pesos on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Bristow, Dow Jones Newswires; +57-314-298-32-77, matthew.bristow@dowjones.com [ 01-12-10 1551ET ]\par \par \uc2 1845 5071   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100112e61c000tt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
